\chapter{One student's work, for \texttt{canvaslms}}
\label{View}

The commands of the previous chapters answer questions about a whole
activity: all reviews, all grades, all threads.
A teacher grading in [[canvaslms]] asks a different question, about one
student at a time: what did this student do?
For an FBF assignment the Canvas submission cannot answer it, since FBF
sends Canvas only a grade, so [[canvaslms submissions view]] shows little
more than the grade and the comments.
This chapter gives [[canvaslms]] the rest: the work the student handed in,
the grade breakdown, the reviews the student got and gave, the reflection
and the discussion posts.

\section{Overview}

[[canvaslms]] knows Canvas, and we know FBF, so the division of labour is
this: [[canvaslms]] hands us a Canvas submission and a directory, and we
hand back a list of [[Section]]s, each a title, a Markdown body and the
files we downloaded.
[[canvaslms]] prints them among its own sections and converts the files as
it converts Canvas attachments.
It never sees an FBF resource, so when FBF changes its API the fix stays
in this package.

The work is done by a [[Viewer]], which [[canvaslms]] creates once per
command and asks once per submission.
Answering one submission takes four steps, each in a section below:
\begin{enumerate}
\item find the FBF activity of the Canvas assignment, launching once per
  course (\cref{ViewLaunch});
\item find the student among the activity's participants
  (\cref{ViewStudent});
\item fetch each part of the student's work, a section each:
  the work handed in (\cref{ViewWork}), the grade (\cref{ViewGrade}),
  the reviews (\cref{ViewReviews}), the reflection
  (\cref{ViewReflection}) and the discussion posts
  (\cref{ViewDiscussions});
\item put the parts together so that one that fails costs only itself
  (\cref{ViewTogether}).
\end{enumerate}

The cost is what shapes the design.
The other commands fetch everything, which for the reviews of one activity
is some 800 requests (\cref{Reviews}).
A view of one submission must instead ask only for what concerns the
student, and a view of many submissions must not repeat what is the same
for all of them: the launch, the activity's tasks and participants, the
participants' email addresses.
So the [[Viewer]] keeps those, and each part filters before it fetches.

