\section{What [[canvaslms]] gets}

A [[Section]] is what [[canvaslms]] prints under one heading.
The body is Markdown, which [[canvaslms]] renders in the terminal or
writes to a file as it is; the [[files]] are paths in the directory that
[[canvaslms]] gave us.
We keep the files apart from the body, rather than inlining them, because
[[canvaslms]] already knows how to turn a file into text: code becomes a
highlighted block, a PDF its text, anything else goes through Pandoc.
Doing that here as well would be a second, worse, copy.

<<imports>>=
import dataclasses
<<classes>>=
@dataclasses.dataclass
class Section:
    """A titled part of a student's FBF work, for `canvaslms` to print."""

    title: str
    body: str = ""
    files: list = dataclasses.field(default_factory=list)
@

The errors that [[canvaslms]] should report, rather than crash on, are
those of a remote service that failed or changed: a launch that broke, a
request that failed, a name FBF no longer has, a kind of data we cannot
read.
They are the errors that our own commands report as messages
(\cref{CLI}), so we export the same tuple.
Any other exception is a bug, here or in the caller, and must stay loud.
The [[Viewer]] below carries them too, as [[errors]], since a caller that
found the class through the import system holds the viewer, not the module.

