\section{The student}
\label{ViewStudent}

FBF does not know the Canvas user id of its users, so we match them as the
grade breakdown does (\cref{Progress}): by email address, which FBF gives
per user and Canvas per student.
The participants are the actors of the activity's progresses, since every
user who opened the activity has a progress.

Asking every participant for their addresses costs one request each, some
150 for a large activity, before anything else can be shown.
We cut that down with the name.
The participants come with their names, so we first ask only those named
like the student, usually one, and the address decides.
Only when none of them matches, because the student uses another name in
FBF, do we ask the rest.
The name only orders the questions; it never decides a match, since two
students may share a name.
The addresses we learn are kept by the [[Viewer]], so a view of a whole
assignment asks each participant at most once.

The result is a set of user ids, not one: a student can have two FBF
identities with the same address, as the grade breakdown found
(\cref{Progress}), and the work of both is the student's.
A student who matches no participant has not opened the activity.

<<constants>>=
NOT_TAKEN_PART = ("The student has not opened this assignment in "
                  "FeedbackFruits.")
<<functions>>=
def student_users(fbf, users, student, emails):
    """Return the ids of the FBF `users` that are the Canvas `student`.

    `users` maps user ids to user resources; `emails` is the address cache
    of `fbf.progress.actor_emails`.  Users named like the student are asked
    for their addresses first, the others only if none of them matches.
    """
    name = folded(getattr(student, "name", None))
    alike = sorted(id for id, user in users.items()
                   if name and folded(attribute(user, "name")) == name)
    for group in (alike, sorted(users)):
        progress_module.actor_emails(fbf, group, emails)
        matched = progress_module.match_actors(
            {id: emails[id] for id in group}, [student])
        if matched:
            return set(matched)
    return set()


def folded(name):
    """Return `name` for comparison: case and spacing ignored."""
    return " ".join((name or "").casefold().split())
@

