\section{The work handed in}
\label{ViewWork}

What a student hands in to an FBF activity is not in the reference
capture of \cref{HAR}, so we found it the way we found the reviews: by
following the relationships from the activity, printing only names.
A peer-feedback activity's [[submit-tasks]] task has a [[submissions]]
relationship that lists all the work handed in, by everyone, in one
request.
Each item is an [[assignments/submission]] with a [[user]], a [[title]], a
[[category]] such as [[document]] or [[raw-file]], a creation time, and a
[[media]] reference to the file itself.
A student may hand in several items, up to three each in the activities we
looked at.

<<constants>>=
SUBMIT_TASK = "submit-tasks"
WORK_TITLE = "FeedbackFruits submission"
<<required names>>=
requires("submit-tasks", relationships=["submissions"])
requires("assignments/submission",
         attributes=["title", "category", "created-at"],
         relationships=["user", "media"])
@

The files are not in the API but in a media service, on the API's host
name with [[media]] for [[api]]
([[media.eu.feedbackfruits.com]] for [[api.eu.feedbackfruits.com]]).
Its [[media]] references cannot be followed as relationships: they have no
link, and the API answers 404 for them.
The service gives, for a media id, a JSON object (not JSON:API) with the
file's [[name]], without extension, and its [[type]], which is the
extension; the file is then at [[<id>/<name>.<type>]], which redirects to
FBF's cloud storage.
Neither request needs a token: the ids are long random UUIDs, which serve
as the credential, as in a shared link.
We still send them through the client's session, so that they go through
the same connection pool.
A few items, three of some 800 we looked at, have an empty [[type]] and no
file at [[<id>/<name>.]] or [[<id>/<name>]]; for those we name the item and
say that there is no file.

<<constants>>=
NO_FILE = "FeedbackFruits has no file for it"
<<imports>>=
import pathlib
import urllib.parse
from fbf.api import APIError, TIMEOUT
from fbf.schema import SchemaError
<<functions>>=
def media_base(api_url):
    """Return the URL of the media service of the FBF API at `api_url`."""
    parts = urllib.parse.urlsplit(api_url)
    host = parts.netloc
    if host.startswith("api."):
        host = "media." + host[len("api."):]
    return f"{parts.scheme}://{host}"


def download_media(session, base, media_id, workdir):
    """Download the file of `media_id` into `workdir`; return its path.

    Returns `None` when the media service has no file for it.
    """
    url = f"{base}/{media_id}"
    info = checked(session.get(url, timeout=TIMEOUT), url).json()
    name, kind = media_field(info, "name"), media_field(info, "type")
    if not kind:
        return None
    url = f"{base}/{media_id}/{urllib.parse.quote(name)}.{kind}"
    response = checked(session.get(url, timeout=TIMEOUT), url)
    workdir = pathlib.Path(workdir)
    workdir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    path = free_path(workdir, f"{pathlib.PurePath(name).name or 'file'}.{kind}")
    path.write_bytes(response.content)
    return path
@

The media service is outside the API's schema checks (\cref{Schema}), so
we check its two names ourselves and fail the same way.
A failed request becomes an [[APIError]] named by the URL we asked for, not
the storage URL we were redirected to, whose query is a signed token.

<<functions>>=
def media_field(info, name):
    """Return the field `name` of a media object; raise `SchemaError`."""
    if name not in info:
        raise SchemaError({"type": "media"}, "field", name)
    return info[name]


def checked(response, url):
    """Return `response` if it succeeded; raise `APIError` for `url`."""
    if not response.ok:
        raise APIError(response.status_code, "GET", url)
    return response
@

Two items may have the same name, two versions of [[lab.py]] say, and the
second must not overwrite the first, so we number the later ones the way
browsers number downloads.

<<functions>>=
def free_path(directory, filename):
    """Return `directory/filename`, numbered if that is taken."""
    path = directory / filename
    stem, suffix = path.stem, path.suffix
    number = 1
    while path.exists():
        number += 1
        path = directory / f"{stem} ({number}){suffix}"
    return path
@

To test the downloads we run a real web server on the local host (rung~2
of the testing ladder): the media service is plain HTTP, and the server
lets the test go through [[requests]] and its redirects, as the program
does, without a recording, which would hold a student's file.
It serves one media object with a file behind a redirect, one with an
empty type, and nothing else.

<<test functions>>=
import http.server
import json
import threading

class MediaHandler(http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler):
  OBJECTS = {"/m1": {"name": "lab", "type": "py", "category": "document"},
             "/m2": {"name": "lost", "type": "", "category": None}}

  def do_GET(self):
    if self.path in self.OBJECTS:
      self.reply(200, json.dumps(self.OBJECTS[self.path]).encode())
    elif self.path == "/m1/lab.py":
      self.send_response(302)
      self.send_header("Location", "/blob/lab.py?sig=secret")
      self.end_headers()
    elif self.path.startswith("/blob/lab.py"):
      self.reply(200, b"print('hello')\n")
    else:
      self.reply(404, b"not found")

  def reply(self, status, body):
    self.send_response(status)
    self.send_header("Content-Length", str(len(body)))
    self.end_headers()
    self.wfile.write(body)

  def log_message(self, *args):
    pass

@pytest.fixture
def media_server():
  server = http.server.ThreadingHTTPServer(("127.0.0.1", 0), MediaHandler)
  thread = threading.Thread(target=server.serve_forever, daemon=True)
  thread.start()
  yield f"http://127.0.0.1:{server.server_address[1]}"
  server.shutdown()
  server.server_close()
@

A file arrives under its own name, a second copy is numbered, a media
without a type gives no file, and an unknown one fails with the URL we
asked for, not the storage URL.

