\section{Against FBF}

The offline tests rest on what we believe FBF returns.
The contract test of \cref{Schema} checks the names we read, given an
example of each type; the submit task already has one (\cref{Progress}),
and the submissions are behind it.

<<example fetchers>>=
from fbf import view as view_module


@example("assignments/submission")
def submission_handed_in(fbf, login):
    for tasks in review_task_sets(fbf, login):
        if view_module.SUBMIT_TASK in tasks:
            items = fbf.get(view_module.related_link(
                tasks[view_module.SUBMIT_TASK], "submissions")).resources
            if items:
                return items[0]
    raise LookupError("no work handed in in the launched course")
@

A recorded test then checks what the view relies on and what holds on any
day: in each peer review of the launched course, every item handed in has
an author, by which we find the student's, and a media reference, by which
we find the file.
It does not download, since a recording would keep the student's file;
the download is tested against the local server above.

<<test functions>>=
from fbf.reviews import peer_review_tasks as review_tasks

@pytest.mark.vcr
def test_every_item_handed_in_has_one_author(fbf):
  checked = 0
  for activity_resource in fbf.get(
      f"v1/courses/{fbf.login.course_uuid}/activities").resources:
    tasks = review_tasks(fbf, activity_resource["id"])
    if not tasks or SUBMIT_TASK not in tasks:
      continue
    items = fbf.get(related_link(tasks[SUBMIT_TASK],
                                 "submissions")).resources
    authors = [related_id(item, "user") for item in items]
    assert all(authors)
    assert all(related_id(item, "media") for item in items)
    checked += 1
  assert checked
